            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "Using Bing grounding, the latest Mars Perseverance rover news is:\n\n- **Perseverance has been studying “Broom Point,” a layered rock sequence on the rim of Jezero Crater.** NASA reports that the rover found evidence that a **245-foot-thick / 75-meter-thick stack of ancient rock** there was built up by **repeated asteroid impacts**, preserving a record of violent events in Mars’ early history【5:1†source】. NASA’s Perseverance news page also lists new imagery and route information for the rover’s trip to Broom Point, including a sweeping view taken by Mastcam-Z【5:2†source】.\n\n- **The rover recently completed a “marathon” on Mars.** Perseverance reached **26.2 miles / 42.195 km** of total driving on its **1,890th Martian day**, after about **five years and four months** on Mars【5:0†source】. That was much faster than Opportunity, which took over 11 years to cover the same distance【5:0†source】.\n\n- **Perseverance is operating beyond Jezero Crater’s western rim.** Recent reporting places the rover west of Jezero in an area nicknamed **“Arbot,”** where orbital imagery from NASA’s Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter/HiRISE captured the rover and its tracks from above【5:0†source】.\n\nIn short: the newest major science update is the **Broom Point impact-record discovery**, while the biggest mission milestone is that Perseverance has now driven a **full marathon distance across Mars**.",
